// `wild:operator-channel` — ADR-0044 V1: the outbound seam Elder uses to
// REACH the operator, and the inbound shape a channel plugin renders.
//
// Direction (read this first — it is INVERSE to `wild:data`):
//
//   - A **channel plugin** (Telegram, Slack, Email, …; Tier-2 Wasm) is the
//     PROVIDER: it `export`s `channel`. The **host** is the CONSUMER: it
//     calls `deliver()` to push a notification out, and `capabilities()`
//     to learn what the surface can render. This mirrors
//     `wild:tool-provider` (guest exports `tools`, host calls them), NOT
//     `wild:data` (host implements, guest imports).
//
//   - Therefore the host-tree world (`operator-channel-host`) is
//     `export channel` — bindgen generates typed `call_deliver` /
//     `call_capabilities` the host invokes against the instantiated guest.
//
// Pull vs push is a capability, not a type (ADR-0044 retires ADR-0019's
// "surfaces are not channels" split): every channel declares
// `channel-kind` via `capabilities()`.
//
//   - `pull` (MCP, `wild watch`): the operator polls the channel's own
//     surface (inbox list, dashboard). `deliver()` updates channel-local
//     state and registers NO delivery-receipt — the operator sees current
//     state on the next poll.
//   - `push` (Telegram, Slack, Email, SMS): the channel actively pings the
//     operator's external endpoint; each `deliver()` returns a
//     `delivery-receipt` so fallback channels know what's already been tried.
//
// V1 status: the in-tree MCP/REST channels are host-NATIVE (not Wasm
// guests) and implement the host-side `OperatorChannel` trait that mirrors
// this contract. This WIT pins the shape the first real Tier-2 PUSH plugin
// (Telegram) will export. A future Telegram guest world composes:
//
//   world telegram-channel-plugin {
//       include wild:plugin-meta/plugin-base@0.3.0;
//       import wild:secrets/store@0.1.0;          // bot token, chat id
//       import wasi:http/outgoing-handler@0.2.0;  // Telegram Bot API
//       export wild:operator-channel/channel@0.1.0;
//   }
//
// That composition lives in the PLUGIN's own wit/world.wit (it pulls in
// foreign packages); this canonical package stays import-free so the
// host-tree copy is a clean symlink, not a trimmed mirror.

package wild:operator-channel@0.1.0;

interface channel {
    /// How severe / time-critical the notification is. Drives ELDER.md
    /// routing (which channels a given severity fans out to).
    enum severity {
        blocker,
        warning,
        info,
        help-needed,
    }

    /// What KIND of operator-facing event this is. Lets a channel render
    /// differently (a help-request wants a reply; a system-notice does not).
    enum kind {
        audit-finding,
        help-request,
        system-notice,
    }

    /// Sensitivity of the body. A push channel over a less-trusted
    /// transport (SMS, third-party chat) MAY drop `tribe-scoped` / `secret`
    /// bodies and send only the `deeplink`.
    enum pii-class {
        public,
        tribe-scoped,
        secret,
    }

    /// What picking an action MEANS, so a channel can map it to its native
    /// affordance (inline button, reply keyword, deep link).
    enum action-semantics {
        fix,
        ack,
        reject,
        answer-option,
        deeplink,
    }

    /// One resolution the operator can pick. `action-id` is what an inbound
    /// reply carries back (it converges on the existing `inbox_resolve` /
    /// `decision_resolve` domain seam — see ADR-0044 §inbound).
    record action {
        action-id: string,
        label: string,
        semantics: action-semantics,
    }

    /// The outbound payload the host hands a channel to render + deliver.
    /// `notification-id` is deterministic over (source-id, state) so a
    /// redelivery is idempotent rather than a duplicate ping.
    record notification {
        notification-id: string,
        tribe-id: string,
        severity: severity,
        kind: kind,
        title: string,
        // Markdown source; the channel renders to its native format
        // (Telegram MarkdownV2, plain-text SMS, MIME HTML, …).
        body-md: string,
        actions: list<action>,
        // `wild://…` URI to the full inbox view; the only thing a
        // pii-restricted push channel is guaranteed to send.
        deeplink: option<string>,
        pii-class: pii-class,
    }

    /// Returned by a PUSH channel on a successful `deliver()`. Pull channels
    /// return this too but the host does not persist it as a receipt.
    record delivery-receipt {
        // Epoch milliseconds (WIT has no timestamp type; ms is the
        // daemon-wide convention).
        delivered-at: u64,
        // For back-correlating an inbound reply to the pushed message.
        channel-message-id: option<string>,
        // The channel had to truncate the body to fit `max-body-chars`.
        rendered-truncated: bool,
    }

    /// pull = MCP / `wild watch`; push = Telegram / Slack / Email / SMS.
    enum channel-kind {
        pull,
        push,
    }

    /// What a channel's surface can render. Elder pre-filters notifications
    /// that would degrade poorly (e.g. inline actions over plain SMS).
    record channel-capabilities {
        kind: channel-kind,
        supports-rich-markdown: bool,
        supports-inline-actions: bool,
        max-body-chars: u32,
        rate-limit-per-minute: option<u32>,
    }

    /// Why a delivery failed. The host's distributor reacts differently:
    /// retry-via-fallback on `unavailable`, give-up on `permanent`,
    /// back-off `n` seconds on `rate-limited`.
    variant channel-error {
        unavailable(string),
        permanent(string),
        rate-limited(u32),
    }

    /// Push one notification to the operator. Idempotent by
    /// `notification.notification-id`.
    ///
    /// `config` is the operator's channel-private binding for THIS
    /// notification's `(channel-token, tribe)` — the host resolves it from
    /// `system/channels.yaml` per delivery (so an operator edit takes effect
    /// with no restart) and passes the matching entry's `config` map through
    /// as key→value pairs. The channel reads its own keys (a push channel:
    /// its recipient, e.g. `operator-chat-id`; secrets stay `wild:secrets`
    /// aliases named here, never values). Empty when the tribe has no binding
    /// for this channel — the channel then reports a skip, never a wrong send.
    deliver: func(n: notification, config: list<tuple<string, string>>) -> result<delivery-receipt, channel-error>;

    /// What this channel surface can render. Host-polled to drive routing.
    capabilities: func() -> channel-capabilities;
}
